Title: Equality or Death
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

INT. WINCHESTER COLORED SCHOOL - COMMENCEMENT DAY (FLASHBACK TO LAST SEQUENCE)

The correspondent of the *Richmond Daily Temps* in the crowd, a copy of Belton's oration changing hands. The congressman speaks highly of it.

SUPER: "THE WINCHESTER SCHOOL HAS FINISHED WITH BOTH BOYS. BERNARD'S MOTHER SENDS HIM TO HARVARD UNIVERSITY. BELTON HAS NO MEANS."

INT. EDITOR KING'S STUDY - RICHMOND - NIGHT

The oration has been printed in *The Temps*. It has created a great sensation. Every newspaper of consequence has reproduced it in full; the leading journals of England have commented on it; the President of the United States has written Belton a letter of congratulation.

[[Editor King]], Mr. V. M. King, has taken the oration home to read to his wife. She meets him at the door; as he kisses her she sees a sober look in his eye.

EDITOR KING
Wife, it has come at last. May the good Lord cease not to watch over our beloved but erring land.

He leads her to his study and reads Belton's oration to her. We do not hear it. We watch her face while he reads.

INT. EDITOR KING'S BEDROOM - MORNING

He sleeps restlessly. His wife stands by the bed, calling him. He was dreaming.

INT. THE DREAM - A DENSE FOREST OF OAKS

Tall, large oaks shower acorns. A drove of fatted swine munch them greedily. They eat so many that they burst open, and from their rotting carcasses fresh oaks spring up and grow with surprising speed. A dark cloud, a terrible hurricane; old and new oaks fight furiously in the storm.

A LOUD VOICE, like unto that of a God:

VOICE
Know ye not that ye are parents and children? Parents, recognize your children. Children, be proud of the parents from whom you spring.

The hurricane ceases. The clouds speed away as if in terror. The oaks grow up together under a sky of purest blue and birds of all kinds build in them and carol the sweetest songs.

INT. EDITOR KING'S STUDY - MORNING

King, cheerful now, sets down his interpretation: the swine the negroes, the oaks the white people, the acorns the doctrine of human liberty preached by Anglo-Saxons. He decides to send to Winchester for Belton.

INT. THE TEMPS - EDITOR KING'S OFFICE - DAY

Belton, summoned, arrives in Richmond within a couple of days. King has him take a seat and asks his history, his training, his plans. Belton wants a college education but has no funds. King offers to pay his way. Belton gladly accepts. King tells him to call at his home that evening at seven o'clock.

EXT. EDITOR KING'S HOUSE - EVENING

Mr. King sits on his front porch between his wife and his aged mother. His two children, a girl and a boy, play on the lawn. Belton is invited, to his surprise, to take a seat. The children come to their father, one on each side, and look from father to Belton with questioning eyes.

King takes a check from his pocket and extends it.

EDITOR KING
Mr. Piedmont, this will carry you through college. I have only one favor to ask of you. In all your dealings with my people recognize the fact that there are two widely separated classes of us, and that there is a good side to the character of the worst class. Always seek for and appeal to that side of their nature.

Belton thanks him feelingly and promises to treasure his words.

SUPER: "A PROMISE HE WILL KEEP. REMEMBER IT."

EXT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - NASHVILLE, TENNESSEE - DAY

A school named for Mrs. Harriet Beecher Stowe, author of "Uncle Tom's Cabin." Five buildings on a ten-acre plot: a dormitory for young men, two for young ladies, a recitation building, and the teachers' mansion. Scarcely more than a normal school with a college department attached.

Belton steps out of a carriage and opens the University gate. He walks in with elastic step, head erect, beneath the arch of the doorway.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GENERAL ASSEMBLY ROOM - SUNDAY NIGHT

About three hundred and sixty students from all parts of the South, young women outnumbering young men two to one. The president, a venerable white minister from the North, discourses on the philanthropic work of the North for the freedmen of the South.

On the wall, a map of the United States with black dots wherever the white friends of the North have planted a school for colored people. Belton sits scrutinizing it.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GENERAL ASSEMBLY ROOM - MONDAY MORNING

The teachers sit in a row across the platform facing the students. On the right side of and next to the president sits a colored man, calm, self-possessed, crossing his legs and stroking his beard.

Belton stares, lips apart, body bent forward.

BELTON
(hunching his seat-mate)
Who is that?

STUDENT
The colored teacher of the faculty.

The teachers gather in a group before dismissal. The colored teacher is in the center, discussing as freely as any, listened to with every mark of respect. His view seems about to prevail. Belton cannot contain himself.

BELTON
Ha! ha!

The whole school turns. The faculty turn. Belton, in a twinkling, is as quiet and solemn as a mouse.

STUDENTS
(tapping their heads)
Wrong here.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GENERAL ASSEMBLY ROOM - LATER (THE SESSION)

The president announces that the faculty meets that evening but that he will be in the city. Belton knows the colored teacher, vice-president of the faculty, will preside. He is determined to see it.

EXT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - DORMITORY - NIGHT

About half past eight, Belton makes a rope of his bedclothes and lets himself down from the second-floor window. Twenty yards away is the "mansion." The mansion dog is Belton's friend; a soft whistle quiets his bark.

EXT. TEACHERS' MANSION - NIGHT

Belton on his knees at the window, which is hoisted, the weather mild. He peeps in. The colored teacher is in the chair in the center of the room. Belton gazes with rapture, unmolested.

A lady teacher rises, tearing a piece of paper, to throw it out the window. She sees the form of a man. She throws up her hands and screams.

Belton flees. The chicken-coop door is open. He jumps in. The chickens set up a cackle; he seizes an old rooster to stop him, misses the throat, and the rooster gives the alarm all the more vociferously. Teachers crowd the window; some men start to the door. Belton decides to play chicken thief and run. He grasps a hen with his other hand, darts out, leaps the college fence at a bound, and wrings off the heads of the chickens to stop the noise.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - TEACHERS' MANSION - LATER

The teachers laugh heartily at their fright. They decide they have been visited by a Negro hunting chickens.

INT. SISTER UNIVERSITY - NASHVILLE - DAY

Belton and another student, sent to carry a note for the president. Through the dining-room window of the sister university he sees its colored teacher eating at the same table with the white teachers.

At Stowe, Belton's own teacher eats with the students. He had not thought of this discrimination before. Now it burns him.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - ASSEMBLY ROOM - NIGHT

Before many days, Belton has called together all the male students.

BELTON
We ought to perfect a secret organization and have a password.

The students agree to secrecy.

BELTON (CONT'D)
"Equality or Death."

He tells them it is his purpose to coerce the white teachers into allowing the colored teacher to eat with them. They agree readily; the matter has been canvassed for sessions but no one dared suggest a combination. Belton is instructed to prepare the complaint, which all agree to sign. A student has a sister among the young ladies; through her Belton's rebellion is organized among the girls and their signatures are secured.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GENERAL ASSEMBLY ROOM - THE EVENTFUL MORNING

The students in their best clothes as though it were the Sabbath. A quiet satisfied look on their faces. The president arrives a little late and finds an official envelope on his desk. He breaks the seal and reads; his color comes and goes. He is nervous through the devotional exercises. He holds his hymn book upside down during the singing. He forgets for some seconds to call on someone to pray.

When the exercises are through he rises with the paper in his hand.

PRESIDENT
I have in my hands a paper from the students of this institution concerning a matter with which they have nothing to do. This is my answer. The classes will please retire.

He gives three strokes to the gong. Not a student moves. A second time. The president, in nervous tones, repeats his assertions and pulls the gong many times in succession. All remain still.

At a signal from Belton, all the students lift their right hands, each bearing a small white board on which is printed in clear type: "Equality or Death."

The president falls back aghast. The white teachers are struck dumb with fear. A number grasp the paper and read it. A hurried consultation; they assure the students the matter shall receive due attention. The president rings the gong again. The students remain.

BELTON
(rising)
It is the determination of the students not to move an inch unless the matter is adjusted here and now.

The faculty beat a hasty retreat and hold up the white flag. They agree that the colored teacher shall eat with them.

The students break forth cheering and flaunt a black flag on which is painted in white letters: "Victory." They march out two by two, singing "John Brown's Body lies mouldering in the grave, and we go marching on."

The teachers remain behind like hens who have lost their broods.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GENERAL ASSEMBLY ROOM - BACCALAUREATE - SUNDAY NIGHT

Belton's last session. The graduating class, twenty-five, fills the front row. [[Dr. Lovejoy]], president of the University, preaches. His text: "The Kingdom of God is within us."

DR. LOVEJOY
Young men, we shall soon push you forth into the midst of a turbulent world, to play such a part as the voice of God may assign you. Remember that the kingdom of God is within you. Do not go forth into the world to demand favors of the world, but go forth to give unto the world. Be strong in your own hearts.

DR. LOVEJOY (CONT'D)
If you heed my voice you shall become true patriots. If you disregard it, you will become time-serving demagogues, playing upon the passions of the people for the sake of short-lived notoriety. Such men would corral all the tigers in the forest and organize them into marauding regiments simply for the honor of being in the lead. Be ye none of these, my boys.

DR. LOVEJOY (CONT'D)
Use not the misfortunes of your people as stones of a monument erected to your name.

Belton listens more attentively than anyone. When the preacher sits he bows his head in his hands; his white locks flutter in the spring breeze. In silence he prays that his work and words not be in vain. The same fervent prayer is on Belton's lips. Somewhere the two prayers meet, lock arms, and go before God together.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - THE MISSISSIPPIAN'S ROOM - NIGHT (WEDNESDAY)

A student noted for his immense height and the size and scent of his feet. When visitors stay too long, he complains of a corn and pulls off a shoe. He is rehearsing his oration when Belton's room-mate enters, takes up a book, and appears absorbed. The Mississippian pulls off one shoe, then the other, then his coat, then undresses and snores. The room-mate seems to pay no attention.

The Mississippian gives up the battle, falls asleep in earnest. Belton's room-mate takes two large new socks from his pocket, puts them in the Mississippian's shoes, and takes the dirty socks.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - BELTON'S ROOM - NIGHT

Belton, valedictorian, has carefully arranged his clothes for tomorrow: a Prince Albert suit of lovely black, and in the tail pocket a most beautiful and costly silk handkerchief, bought lest he weep in the valedictory. He has gone to bed early.

His room-mate, insanely jealous, takes the handkerchief and puts the dirty socks in its place.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - ASSEMBLY ROOM - COMMENCEMENT DAY - THURSDAY

The room decorated like the Garden of Eden. Visitors in droves. [[Editor King]] is present. Hannah has saved her scant earnings and come to see her son. A tiny white bud on his black coat from the fair maiden who has sent it.

Belton delivers his oration and the audience cheers wildly for many minutes. He then delivers the valedictory to the class. His voice begins to break with emotion. At the most affecting portion he reaches into his coat tail pocket for the handkerchief. A smile is on well-nigh every face in the audience. Belton, head bowed, does not see.

He lifts the "handkerchief" to his eyes. The socks come near his nose. He stops. He holds them at arm's length. He flings them in front of him; they fall on some students, who brush them off. The house is in an uproar.

BELTON
Ladies and gentlemen, these socks are from Mississippi. I am from Virginia.

An even louder outburst. Belton bows, leaves the platform murmuring that he will find and kill the rascal who played the trick.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - BOYS' DORMITORY - DAY

He runs to the city and buys a pistol. He comes back at a furious pace. No one dares approach him. The president meets him at the dormitory door, places a hand on his shoulder; Belton roughly pushes him aside and searches the building for his room-mate, who has already left.

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - BELTON'S ROOM - NEXT MORNING

The president enters early and takes Belton's hand.

DR. LOVEJOY
(showing him what an unholy passion revenge is)
Belton, you are about to allow a pair of dirty socks to wreck your whole life.

He draws a picture of the suffering Savior, crying out between darting pains:

DR. LOVEJOY (CONT'D)
Lord forgive them for they know not what they do.

Belton is melted to tears of repentance. A cardinal principle of his life: to allow God to avenge all his wrongs.

EXT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - GIRLS' DORMITORY - MONDAY

Belton has one dollar and a quarter left after his graduation expenses and his ticket home. A young lady friend stands on the steps with three trunks. A crowd of girls to bid her adieu. He asks the cabman his fee.

CABMAN
For you and the young lady and the trunks, two dollars, sir.

Belton almost freezes. Affecting indifference:

BELTON
What is the price for the young lady and the trunks?

CABMAN
One dollar and fifty cents. You can call any hackman you choose and you won't find one who will do it for a cent less.

EXT. RAILWAY STATION - DAY

He sees her into the coach. Behind the station house, out of sight of the train, he beckons the driver.

BELTON
Friend, here is one dollar and a quarter. It is all I have. Trust me for the balance until tomorrow.

CABMAN
Oh! no. I must have my money to-day. I have to report to-night and my money must go in. Just fork over the balance, please. I am going to the young lady.

The driver hears the train blow and endeavors to pass. Belton grasps him by the collar, trips him, and falls astride of him. The train pulls out. Belton jumps up and runs to wave good-bye.

INT. POLICE COURT - LATER

The police justice fines him ten dollars. A crowd of white men who heard Belton's story, admiring his respect for the girl, pay the fine and make up a purse.

At Stowe, Belton learned to respect women.

CUT TO:
